{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.ParityModulatedRatio","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.ParityModulatedRatio","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_const_imp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_const_imp","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ParityModulatedRatio.lean#L149-L159","doc":"**A one-step ratio that is constant in `m` forces the modulation away.**\n\nContrapositive of the above, and the sanity check on the whole diagnosis: if\nthe one-step ratio really had settled to a single branch-independent limit,\nthe parity amplitude would have to be trivial. Observing a stable 2-cycle is\ntherefore incompatible with \"the estimator converged\", however stable\nconsecutive same-parity samples look. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_ne_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_ne_iff","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ParityModulatedRatio.lean#L145-L147","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_eq_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_eq_iff","kind":"theorem","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ParityModulatedRatio.lean#L129-L143","doc":"The 2-cycle is **visible exactly when `α ^ 2 ≠ β ^ 2`.** At `α = ±β` the\nmodulation is invisible to the one-step ratio and no spurious branch-dependence\nappears at all — the estimator is only misleading off that balance. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_mul_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_mul_eq","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ParityModulatedRatio.lean#L118-L127","doc":"**The punchline.** The two phases of the one-step ratio multiply to `γ ^ 2`:\nthe branch datum enters one as `β/α` and the other as `α/β`, and cancels.\n\nSo the geometric mean of the two phases recovers `γ` exactly, for every branch.\nThat is why averaging the parities is the *canonical* repair of the estimator\nrather than an ad-hoc patch — and why a single-parity sample cannot be fixed by\ngoing to larger `m`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_odd","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ParityModulatedRatio.lean#L107-L116","doc":"At **odd** `m` the one-step ratio is `γ * (α/β)` — the same branch datum,\ninverted. Together with `oneStepRatio_even` this is the 2-cycle. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.oneStepRatio_even","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ParityModulatedRatio.lean#L97-L105","doc":"At **even** `m` the one-step ratio is `γ * (β/α)` — it carries the branch\ndatum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.twoStepRatio_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.twoStepRatio_eq","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ParityModulatedRatio.lean#L80-L95","doc":"**The two-step ratio is `γ ^ 2`, always.**\n\nNo hypothesis relates `α` and `β`: the branch datum cancels identically, at\nevery `m`, for both parities. This is the exact statement behind\n\"the parity-free radius is branch-independent\" — and it is an identity, not a\nlimit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.parityGeom_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.parityGeom_odd","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ParityModulatedRatio.lean#L76-L78","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.parityGeom_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.parityGeom_even","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ParityModulatedRatio.lean#L72-L74","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.parityGeom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ParityRatio.parityGeom","kind":"def","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ParityModulatedRatio.lean#L65-L70","doc":"A geometric sequence whose amplitude alternates with the parity of the\nindex: `α` on even indices, `β` on odd. The two-step decoupling of a recurrence\ncoupling `m` to `m + 2` produces this shape, with `α/β` fixed by the initial\nconditions — so `α/β` is the *branch* datum. ","signatureChunk":0}]}